\begin_layout Standard
The aforementioned implicit time integrators require the solution to the linear system
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:newtons-method-2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 recast here as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Ax=b.\label{eq:matrix_example_1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

It is well-established that employing a direct solver requires 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 operations for an 
\begin_inset Formula $n\times n$
\end_inset

 square matrix
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Gol13"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Flex PDF-Markup
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For example,
 backward Euler may require around five linear solves per time step to converge the Newton iteration.
 In comparison,
 SDIRK-2 and SDIRK-4 require a Newton solve per stage,
 which by the same example is roughly 10 and 25 linear solves per step,
 respectively.
 This makes Rosenbrock and YASS appealing time integration strategies,
 as they eliminate the need for a full Newton solve.
 Rosenbrock methods typically require two linear solves per step,
 while YASS,
 without adaptation,
 requires only one.
\begin_inset Argument post:1
status open

