\begin_layout Plain Layout
In computational physics,
 The optimal choice of time integrator is problem dependent.
 For unsteady simulations requiring extremely small time steps,
 such as detonations,
 the YASS integrator is often sufficient,
 offering a balance of stability,
 accuracy,
 and computational efficiency.
 In contrast,
 higher-order methods like SDIRK-4 may be more suitable for steady-state or low Mach number solvers,
 where larger time steps are feasible.
 Although lower-order integrators are generally faster per step,
 they may necessitate sub-stepping or adaptive routines to maintain stability and accuracy,
 introducing additional complexity that may not be desirable in all applications.
